{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"RDSN stands for Recurrent Deep Stacking Networks, applied to Robust Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) tasks.","source_span":"applying Recurrent Deep Stacking Networks (RDSNs) to Robust Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) tasks"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"Bi-Pass Stacking Network (BPSN) is proposed as a more efficient yet comparable substitute to RDSN.","source_span":"we also proposed a more efficient yet comparable substitute to RDSN, Bi- Pass Stacking Network (BPSN)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"The main idea of RDSN/BPSN is to add phoneme-level information into acoustic models, transforming an acoustic model into a combination of an acoustic model and a phoneme-level N-gram model.","source_span":"The main idea of these two models is to add phoneme-level information into acoustic models, transforming an acoustic model to the combination of an acoustic model and a phoneme-level N-gram model"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"Experiments were conducted on the CHiME-4 dataset using all single channel utterances, totaling around 100 hours.","source_span":"We conducted experiments on CHiME-4 dataset, using all of the single channel utterances. The total length of the dataset is around 100 hours."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"The training set has 8738 utterances, the development set has 3280 utterances, and the test set has 2640 utterances.","source_span":"has 8738 utterances. The development set is consisted of 3280 utterances and test set 2640"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"For GMM-HMM model training and decoding, the recipe in Kaldi was used.","source_span":"For GMM-HMM model training and decoding, we used the recipe in Kaldi."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"The baseline model was a conventional DNN with 6 hidden layers of 1024 nodes each, using exponential ReLUs and dropout.","source_span":"the baseline was a conventional DNN with 6 hidden layers, exponential Rectified Linear Units (ReLUs), and drop out. Each hidden layer has 1024 nodes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"The baseline used 40-dimensional MFCC input features, concatenating 9 previous and 9 following frames to form a 760-dimensional input vector, with a 3161-dimensional output.","source_span":"The input features extracted from the utterances were 40 dimensional MFCC features. We concatenated 9 features from previous frames and 9 from the following frames, forming a 760 dimensional input vector. The output has 3161 dimensions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"For both RDSN and BPSN, 9 previous outputs were taken as additional inputs, forming a 1138-dimensional input vector, initialized from the 15th epoch of the baseline model.","source_span":"For both RDSN and BPSN, we took 9 previous outputs as additional inputs, forming a 1138 dimensional vector...We used the 15th epoch of the baseline model as the initial models to RDSN and BPSN."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","statement":"The compression method reduces the output dimension from thousands to about forty by summing values with the same corresponding monophone.","source_span":"This process compresses the dimension from thousands to about forty."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[0,1,2],"question":"What are RDSN and BPSN, and what is the main idea behind them?","answer":"RDSN (Recurrent Deep Stacking Network) and BPSN (Bi-Pass Stacking Network, a more efficient substitute to RDSN) are models applied to Robust ASR. Their main idea is to add phoneme-level information into acoustic models, turning the acoustic model into a combination of an acoustic model and a phoneme-level N-gram model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What dataset was used for the experiments and how long is it?","answer":"The experiments used the CHiME-4 dataset with all single channel utterances, totaling around 100 hours."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How many utterances were in the training, development, and test sets?","answer":"The training set had 8738 utterances, the development set had 3280 utterances, and the test set had 2640 utterances."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What was the architecture of the baseline DNN model?","answer":"The baseline was a conventional DNN with 6 hidden layers of 1024 nodes each, using exponential ReLUs and dropout."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What were the input and output dimensions of the baseline model's feature vectors?","answer":"The baseline used 40-dimensional MFCC features, concatenating 9 previous and 9 following frames to form a 760-dimensional input vector, with an output of 3161 dimensions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How many previous outputs did RDSN and BPSN use as additional inputs, and what was the resulting input vector size?","answer":"RDSN and BPSN took 9 previous outputs as additional inputs, forming a 1138-dimensional input vector, and were initialized from the 15th epoch of the baseline model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0106","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How much does the compression method reduce the output dimensionality in RDSN/BPSN?","answer":"The compression method reduces the output dimension from thousands down to about forty."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"The approach was tested in eight different languages: Spanish, English, Italian, Arabic, German, Portuguese, Russian and Swedish.","source_span":"we propose a robust multilingual sentiment analysis method, tested in eight different languages: Spanish, English, Italian, Arabic, German, Portuguese, Russian and Swedish"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"The approach was compared against three international contests: TASS'15 (Spanish), SemEval'15-16 (English), and SENTIPOLC'14 (Italian).","source_span":"We compare our approach ranking in three international contests: TASS'15, SemEval'15-16 and SENTIPOLC'14, for Spanish, English and Italian respectively"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"B4MSA (baseline algorithm for multilingual Sentiment Analysis) was designed to be multilingual and easy to implement.","source_span":"this baseline algorithm for multilingual Sentiment Analysis (B4MSA) was designed with the purpose of being multilingual and easy to implement"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"B4MSA's pipeline applies text-transformations to messages, represents the transformed text in a vector space model, and finally classifies using a Support Vector Machine with linear kernel.","source_span":"B4MSA starts by applying text-transformations to the messages, then transformed text is represented in a vector space model...finally, a Support Vector Machine (with linear kernel) is used as the classifier"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"B4MSA uses a simple random search and hill-climbing method in the space of text-transformations to select the best-performing ones.","source_span":"B4MSA uses a simple random search and hill-climbing (see Subsection SECREF14 ) in space of text-transformations to free the user from this delicate and time-consuming task"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"To maintain the cross-language property, B4MSA does not use additional knowledge such as affective lexicons or models based on distributional semantics.","source_span":"we limit ourselves to not use additional knowledge, this include knowledge from affective lexicons or models based on distributional semantics"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","statement":"Symbol reduction (del-d1) is a parameter addressing the strategy of replacing repeated characters that Twitter users use to emphasize parts of words.","source_span":"Symbol reduction (del-d1), usually, twitter messages use repeated characters to emphasize parts of the word to attract user's attention...We applied the strategy of replacing the r"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","fact_refs":[0],"question":"In how many languages was the multilingual sentiment analysis approach (B4MSA) tested, and which languages?","answer":"It was tested in eight languages: Spanish, English, Italian, Arabic, German, Portuguese, Russian, and Swedish."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which three international contests were used to benchmark the approach, and for which languages?","answer":"TASS'15 for Spanish, SemEval'15-16 for English, and SENTIPOLC'14 for Italian."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the three main steps in the B4MSA pipeline?","answer":"B4MSA applies text-transformations to messages, represents the transformed text in a vector space model, and classifies it using a Support Vector Machine with a linear kernel."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does B4MSA select the best text-transformations for a given dataset?","answer":"It uses a simple random search combined with hill-climbing in the space of text-transformations."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Does B4MSA use external knowledge sources like affective lexicons?","answer":"No, B4MSA avoids additional knowledge such as affective lexicons or distributional semantics models in order to maintain its cross-language property."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0107","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What does the symbol reduction (del-d1) parameter in B4MSA address?","answer":"It addresses the tendency of Twitter messages to use repeated characters to emphasize parts of a word, by applying a strategy of replacing the repeated characters."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","statement":"Trolling is defined as the activity of posting messages via a communications network that are intended to be provocative, offensive or menacing.","source_span":"Trolling is “the activity of posting messages via a communications network that are intended to be provocative, offensive or menacing” BIBREF0"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","statement":"The proposed trolling categorization characterizes conversations using four aspects: the troll's intention, the troll's intention disclosure, the responder's interpretation of the troll's intention, and the responder's response strategy.","source_span":"characterizing these two perspectives using four aspects, namely, the troll's intention and his intention disclosure, as well as the responder's interpretation of the troll's intention and her response strategy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","statement":"The annotated dataset released in this paper contains excerpts of Reddit conversations involving suspected trolls and their interactions with other users.","source_span":"we annotate and release a dataset containing excerpts of Reddit conversations involving suspected trolls and their interactions with other users"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","statement":"According to hardaker2010trolling, a troll's real intention is to cause disruption and/or trigger or exacerbate conflict for the purpose of their own amusement.","source_span":"a troll's “real intention(s) is/are to cause disruption and/or trigger or exacerbate conflict for the purpose of their own amusement”"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","statement":"mihaylov2015finding addressed identifying manipulation trolls in news community forums, basing predictions solely on non-linguistic information such as number of votes, dates, and number of comments.","source_span":"mihaylov2015finding address the problem of identifying manipulation trolls in news community forums...all their predictions are based on non-linguistic information such as number of votes, dates, number of comments and so on"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","statement":"kumar2014accurately and guha2004propagation identify malicious individuals in a network based solely on the network's properties rather than on the textual content of comments.","source_span":"kumar2014accurately and guha2004propagation present a methodology to identify malicious individuals in a network based solely on the network's properties rather than on the textual content of comments"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How is trolling defined in this paper?","answer":"Trolling is defined as the activity of posting messages via a communications network that are intended to be provocative, offensive, or menacing."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What four aspects does the paper's trolling categorization use to characterize conversations?","answer":"It uses the troll's intention, the troll's intention disclosure, the responder's interpretation of the troll's intention, and the responder's response strategy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the source of the annotated dataset created for this trolling research?","answer":"It consists of excerpts of Reddit conversations involving suspected trolls and their interactions with other users."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","fact_refs":[3],"question":"According to hardaker2010trolling, what is a troll's real intention?","answer":"To cause disruption and/or trigger or exacerbate conflict for the purpose of their own amusement."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What kind of information did mihaylov2015finding use to identify manipulation trolls in news community forums?","answer":"They based all predictions on non-linguistic information such as number of votes, dates, and number of comments, rather than the textual content."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0108","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What did kumar2014accurately and guha2004propagation base their malicious individual identification on?","answer":"They identified malicious individuals based solely on the network's properties, rather than on the textual content of comments."}
